Aether: protection panels and layers

Shields panel, trust reroute, known-fingerprinter blocking, stock UA, top-load keying, GL baselines, touch/fonts/language/depth/timezone normalization.

Squashed from 11 units:

  [core/122-aether-shields-panel.patch]
  Aether: own shields panel replacing the protections popup
  
  The shield button now opens our own slim shields panel instead of the
  Firefox protections popup: master toggle, per-site exemption, a reload
  prompt after any change, and a link into the full fingerprint settings
  (Standard/Strict/Custom). Layer toggles live in about:aethersettings
  and the stock popups, not here. New template, controller, strings and
  styles; old popup stays in tree unused (later cleanup).
  This unit originally retired ETP underneath the panel (blocking families
  off, both modes). The 0.1 stack drops that override again: stock Enhanced
  Tracking Protection stays exactly as Firefox ships it. Cookie
  partitioning, query stripping and bounce protection were never touched.
  Gate: headed visual by the reporter (panel opens, master flips pref,
  settings link opens, broken-site exempts), zero chrome errors, full gates.
  
  [core/123-trust-panel-reroute.patch]
  Aether: route the trust panel button to our shields
  
  The previous unit rerouted the shield button, but the screenshot proved
  a second entry point: the trust panel ("Aether is on guard") opens from
  the trust icon and shows the same retired ETP content. Reroute its
  handler to our shields panel too, so every site-info button lands on
  ours. Old popups stay in tree unused (later cleanup).
  
  [core/124-block-known-fingerprinters.patch]
  Aether: block known fingerprinters from a vendored snapshot
  
  Aether: block known fingerprinters from a vendored snapshot
  
  Disconnect's open tracking-protection list, categories
  FingerprintingGeneral + FingerprintingInvasive (556 domains), frozen as a
  static set inside the matcher module. No list updates at runtime, no
  classifier surgery: a small system observer cancels third-party loads from
  listed hosts before they run. First-party loads always pass; undeterminable
  relationships fail open. Own master pref (default on) plus a test-only
  extra-hosts pref; own toggle at the top of Settings > Privacy & Security.
  
  [core/125-stock-firefox-ua.patch]
  Aether: stock Firefox UA unless the user opts into the app token
  
  Aether: stock Firefox UA unless the user opts into the app token
  
  The trailing Aether/156.0.1 token is ~17 stable bits while our user base is
  small. BuildUserAgent now skips the app portion unless
  general.useragent.showAppToken is true (any general.useragent.* change
  rebuilds the UA live). appVersion already reports the legacy stock value;
  platform/oscpu/buildID stay coherent.
  
  [core/126-top-load-keying.patch]
  Aether: key noise on the top load, first-party exemptions only
  
  Aether: key noise on the top load, first-party exemptions only
  
  Frame-origin keying let a tracker framed on several sites read identical
  noise everywhere (linkable). Noise keys now come from the propagated
  top-load key (generated per top document load when RFP leaves none, same
  inheritance pipe, cross-process included; reload re-keys by design), with
  legacy per-base keying only where no document exists (workers). Exemptions
  cover first-party reads of the exempted site only; third-party frames stay
  noised. Supersedes the old peer-differs P1 gate with a linkability gate:
  two tops plus the same tracker framed in both.
  
  [core/127-gl-caps-baseline.patch]
  Aether: coherent WebGL caps baseline, rare extensions hidden, WebGPU off
  
  Aether: coherent WebGL caps baseline, rare extensions hidden, WebGPU off
  
  Renderer strings alone still let MAX_* limits, extension lists and
  precision reports describe the real GPU. Numeric caps now report safe
  baselines when WebGL protection applies (texture/cube/renderbuffer 8192,
  uniform vectors 256, varyings 16, viewport 16384; under-reporting is the
  safe direction), and rare high-entropy extensions stay hidden
  (WEBGL_debug_shaders leaks translated sources; WEBGL_provoking_vertex and
  OVR_multiview2 are Mozilla/VR-only tells; internal slots stay empty so
  dependent paths agree). WebGPU ships adapter info past everything, so
  dom.webgpu.enabled defaults off (user-overridable).
  
  [core/128-normalize-touch-points.patch]
  Aether: normalize touch points with a Settings toggle
  
  Aether: normalize touch points with a Settings toggle
  
  Touch laptops report rare maxTouchPoints values (7+ bits on CoverYourTracks).
  Report 0 behind master + kill prefs with the usual per-site exemption, plus
  its own toggle next to the fingerprinter switch at the top of Settings >
  Privacy & Security.
  
  [core/129-restrict-page-fonts.patch]
  Aether: restrict page-visible fonts with a Settings toggle
  
  Aether: restrict page-visible fonts with a Settings toggle
  
  Font presence lists are ~5 stable bits. Page CSS font matching is now
  restricted behind a master + kill pref pair: documents resolve at Gecko's
  base-system visibility level, and on top of that Aether hides the Windows
  families that carry edition/Office signal but no glyph coverage of their
  own (decorative and bundled-Office families only -- CJK, Indic, Thai,
  symbol-coverage and locale fonts are deliberately left visible so text
  never falls back to tofu). Chrome, devtools, RFP-exempt documents and
  Aether-exempted sites resolve at a higher level and are unaffected.
  OffscreenCanvas contexts inherit through the standard provider chain.
  
  Own toggle next to the other switches at the top of Settings > Privacy &
  Security. Webfonts via @font-face keep working; only system-font probing
  shrinks.
  
  Hardening from a user log review: chrome documents are never
  restricted (same Rule 2 as the stock mechanism) after the UI icon
  fonts (MDL2, Fluent) showed up blocked; content behavior unchanged.
  
  [core/130-single-page-language.patch]
  Aether: single-entry language list and Accept-Language with a Settings toggle
  
  Aether: single-entry language list and Accept-Language with a Settings toggle
  
  Secondary languages are stable cross-site linkers: rare combinations are
  near-unique while adding nothing to content negotiation. navigator.languages
  is now truncated to the top entry behind master + kill prefs (navigator.language
  is languages[0], so it stays coherent automatically), and the HTTP
  Accept-Language header is cut to its first token at the channel layer, where
  redirects re-enter per hop and every hop is re-evaluated against its own
  target. Workers share one global list and cannot prove first-party off-thread,
  so they truncate without exemption. An explicit language override (containers)
  always wins. Third-party targets always truncate, so a per-site exemption
  never leaks the full list to embedded trackers; first-party targets keep the
  full list only where the top site is exempted. Own toggle next to the other
  switches at the top of Settings > Privacy & Security. DNT stays off
  (deprecated; sending it is itself a minority signal) and GPC is left at its
  stock default for the same reason: no change either way.
  
  [core/131-stock-screen-depth.patch]
  Aether: stock 24-bit screen depth with a Settings toggle
  
  Aether: stock 24-bit screen depth with a Settings toggle
  
  Real hardware is 24-bit almost everywhere, so this is ~0 stable bits on
  typical setups; the setups that are not (16-bit RDP/VM sessions) are a
  telling minority worth covering. screen.colorDepth and screen.pixelDepth
  (ColorDepth delegates to PixelDepth) report 24 behind master + kill prefs,
  with the usual per-site exemption restoring the real depth. Nothing derives
  layout from this value, so there is no invariant to maintain. Own toggle
  next to the other switches at the top of Settings > Privacy & Security.
  
  Deliberately out of scope in this unit: screen geometry and devicePixelRatio.
  Monitor size is entangled with the real window size through hard invariants
  (innerWidth <= screen.width, fullscreen inner == screen > avail); any lie big
  enough to matter breaks them detectably unless innerWidth and media-query
  evaluation are forked too, which is Tor-letterboxing complexity, not a layer.
  CoverYourTracks itself observes screen size varies between visits, so its
  stable linking value is lower than its bit count suggests.
  
  [core/132-utc-timezone-optin.patch]
  Aether: UTC timezone opt-in with a Settings toggle
  
  Aether: UTC timezone opt-in with a Settings toggle
  
  The OS timezone is ~6 stable bits that never change on a machine. Behind
  master + an opt-in pref (off by default), page Date/Intl and dedicated
  workers report UTC instead: window globals take it at global creation from
  the same realm-override seam automation uses (explicit overrides still win,
  chrome globals keep the real zone), and document-created workers take it at
  creation on the parent thread where prefs are readable. Nested workers
  inherit it from their parent. The usual per-site exemption restores the real
  zone where local time matters. Own toggle at the top of Settings > Privacy &
  Security. Residual: service workers have no document on their creation path
  and keep the OS zone.
